1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Where should a new cooling cavity go?
Predict the benefit before making the hole.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Switch on the same heat source in both designs.
The exterior and cavity walls stay cold.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
An auxiliary heat-equation simulation shows the difference.
TWoS optimizes the steady-state design.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Separate 3D examples: a capsule and the Bunny.
Every walk sphere stays inside its domain.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Sample uniform sphere exits.
The lines show jumps, not Brownian paths.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Repeat with independent random seeds.
Here the compliance adjoint is p = u.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Temperature and objective influence predict
the effect of a small new cavity.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Keep one temperature estimate.
Hold out its matching adjoint and average the others.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Multiply the independent factors, then average.
The products themselves can share samples.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Score feasible sites in the current geometry.
Rescore a shortlist with fresh samples.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Insert the selected cavity and update the domain.
The cooling animation shows thermal relaxation.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The percentages remain the paper’s
steady-state benchmark estimates.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
With the same six-hole budget, TWoS gives
47.8% lower heat objective in this retained run.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Random placement gives 39.5%;
grid-based adjoint placement gives 48.1%.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Six larger cavities: 47.4% lower heat objective.
Twelve smaller cavities: 47.1%.

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The twelve smaller cavities remove
half as much material as the six larger ones.

17
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Walking into Holes.
Monte Carlo topological derivatives for PDE design.
